For every $\delta>0$, there is a finite constant $C_\delta$ such that $$\norm{T_\delta f}_{L^3(\mathbb R^3)}
 \le C_\delta\norm f_{L^3(\mathbb R^3)}
 \qquad(f\in\mathcal S(\mathbb R^3)).
 \label{eq:main-bound}$$ Consequently $T_\delta$ has a unique bounded extension to $L^3(\mathbb R^3)$.

Let $A,B,D,T$ be finite observations. Then $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathsf I_{\mathrm{nat}}(T;B)&\le\mathsf I_{\mathrm{nat}}(T;A)+\mathsf H_{\mathrm{nat}}(B\mid A),\label{eq:entropy-determination}\\
 |\mathsf H_{\mathrm{nat}}(B\mid T)-\mathsf H_{\mathrm{nat}}(A\mid T)|
 &\le\mathsf H_{\mathrm{nat}}(B\mid A)+\mathsf H_{\mathrm{nat}}(A\mid B).\label{eq:entropy-change}
\end{aligned}$$ If, outside an event of probability $\zeta$, $B$ has at most $N$ possible values given $A$, and $|\mathcal B|\le M$, then $$\mathsf H_{\mathrm{nat}}(B\mid A)\le\log2+\log N+\zeta\log M.
 \label{eq:entropy-ambiguity}$$ For nonnegative numbers $m_b$ with $m_b>0$ on the support of $B$, $$\mathsf H_{\mathrm{nat}}(B)+\mathbb E\log m_B\le\log\sum_bm_b.
 \label{eq:entropy-masses}$$ Finally, let $\mathsf P,\mathsf Q$ be laws on the same space with $\mathsf Q\le K\mathsf P$, $K\ge1$. For arbitrary $B,D$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathsf H_{\mathrm{nat}}^{\mathsf Q}(D\mid B)
 &=\mathbb E_{\mathsf Q}[-\log\mathsf P(D\mid B)]
   -\mathbb E_{\mathsf Q_B}
     \mathop{\mathrm{KL}}(\mathsf Q_{D\mid B}\Vert\mathsf P_{D\mid B}),\label{eq:conditional-kl}\\
 \mathbb E_{\mathsf Q_B}
     \mathop{\mathrm{KL}}(\mathsf Q_{D\mid B}\Vert\mathsf P_{D\mid B})
 &\le\log K.\label{eq:conditional-kl-bound}
\end{aligned}$$ In particular, restriction to an event of $\mathsf P$-mass $m>0$ costs at most $\log(1/m)$ in <a href="#eq:conditional-kl-bound" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:conditional-kl-bound">[eq:conditional-kl-bound]</a>. None of these estimates depends on the cardinality of $B$ or $T$.

Suppose $f$ is locally Lipschitz on $\mathcal D$, and, in its interior, $$0\le f_j\le d_j,\qquad 0\le f_v\le d_v,
 \qquad f_{jk}\le0\ (j\ne k),\qquad f_{jv}\le0$$ in the distributional sense. The derivative bounds may instead be local bounds on each compact neighborhood under consideration; in that case all conclusions concerning boundedness are local. Then there are, up to null sets, uniquely determined locally bounded fields $a_1,\ldots,a_n,D$ on $\mathbb R\times I$, with $0\le a_j\le d_j$ and $0\le D\le d_v$, having the following properties.*


1.  **Strong traces.* For almost every interior offset $\eta\in\mathcal K^\circ$, the restrictions of the ambient gradients to $\Pi(\cdot)+\eta$ are defined almost everywhere. As $\eta\to0$ through such offsets, they converge strongly in $L^1_{\rm loc}(di\,du)$ to $(a_1,\ldots,a_n,D)$. For $Q\Subset Q'\Subset\mathbb R\times I$ this convergence has the bound $$\label{eq:trace-quantitative-slices}
     \|f_\alpha(\Pi(\cdot)+\eta)-A_\alpha\|_{L^1(Q)}
     \le C_Q|\eta|+\omega_{\alpha,Q'}(C_Q|\eta|),
     \qquad A=(a_1,\ldots,a_n,D),$$ where $\omega_{\alpha,Q'}(r)\to0$ is the $L^1$ translation modulus of $A_\alpha$ on $Q'$. The constant depends only on the local derivative bounds, the clock speeds, and the two patches.*





2.  **Measure identities.* There exist locally finite nonnegative measures $\nu_{jk}=\nu_{kj}$, $j\ne k$, and $\nu_{jv}$ on the plane such that $$\label{eq:trace-mixed-measures}
     V_j a_j=-\nu_{jv}-\sum_{k\ne j}(c_k-c_j)\nu_{jk},
     \qquad \partial_iD=-\sum_j\nu_{jv}.$$ For $F=f\circ\Pi$, the weak derivatives are $$\label{eq:trace-plane-derivatives}
     F_i=\sum_j a_j,\qquad F_u=\sum_j c_ja_j+D.$$ In particular, $$\label{eq:trace-distribution-inequality}
     V_n a_n\ge\partial_iD$$ as measures. With $q_j=1+c_n-c_j$, the full flux identity is $$\label{eq:trace-measure-flux}
     -\sum_jq_jV_ja_j
     =\sum_{j<k}(c_k-c_j)^2\nu_{jk}+\sum_jq_j\nu_{jv}.$$*


3.  **Affine approximation.* There is a sequence $h_\ell\downarrow0$ and a single null set $N\subset\mathbb R\times I$ such that, for every $(i,u)\notin N$ and every finite $R$, $$\label{eq:trace-affine-blowup}
     \lim_{\ell\to\infty}\sup_{\xi\in\mathcal K,\ |\xi|\le R}
     \left|
     \frac{f(\Pi(i,u)+h_\ell\xi)-f(\Pi(i,u))}{h_\ell}
          -\sum_j a_j(i,u)\xi_j-D(i,u)\xi_v
     \right|=0.$$ Only sufficiently large $\ell$, for which the displayed label times belong to $I$, are used. The sequence may depend on $f$.

Assume Theorem <a href="#thm:characteristic-trace" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:characteristic-trace">26</a>, and let $f_\epsilon\to f$ locally uniformly on $\mathcal D$ along the sequence of finite systems under consideration. Fix a bounded measurable base patch $Q$ of positive measure with compact closure in $\mathbb R\times I$, a radius $R\ge1$, an error $\eta>0$, an exceptional proportion $0<\tau<1$, and a maximum block length $h_*>0$. There exist $$0<h<h_*,\qquad G\subset Q,\qquad |Q\setminus G|\le\tau|Q|,$$ and a compact neighborhood $K_0\subset\mathcal D$ containing all $\Pi(q)+h\xi$ with $q\in\overline Q$, $\xi\in\mathcal K\cap B_R$, such that the following holds. If $$\label{eq:trace-finite-tolerance}
 \|f_\epsilon-f\|_{L^\infty(K_0)}\le\eta h/4,$$ then, simultaneously for every $q\in G$ and every $\xi\in\mathcal K\cap B_R$, $$\label{eq:trace-finite-affine}
 |f_\epsilon(\Pi(q)+h\xi)-f_\epsilon(\Pi(q))-hA(q)\cdot\xi|
 \le\eta h.$$ In particular, for every finite family of such displacements, every information increment between two of them differs from its affine value by at most $2\eta h$. An alternating sum of $r$ profile values whose affine contributions cancel has absolute value at most $r\eta h$.*

*All dependencies have the order $$f,\ Q,R,\eta,\tau,h_*\quad\longrightarrow\quad h,G,K_0
 \quad\longrightarrow\quad\epsilon_0.$$ For every member of the converging finite sequence with $\epsilon<\epsilon_0$, <a href="#eq:trace-finite-tolerance" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:trace-finite-tolerance">[eq:trace-finite-tolerance]</a> holds. If finitely many entropy identities also incur a total normalized ambiguity error at most $B/\log(1/\epsilon)$, one may, after choosing $h$, require in addition $$\log(1/\epsilon)\ge B/(\eta h).$$ Each such combined identity then has at most one additional $\eta h$ of error. More generally one may prescribe any sequences $\eta_\ell,\tau_\ell\downarrow0$ and $h_{*,\ell}\downarrow0$ and select successively $h_\ell<h_{*,\ell}$, then the inner approximation thresholds. If $\sum_\ell\tau_\ell<\infty$, almost every point of $Q$ belongs to all but finitely many of the resulting $G_\ell$.

Let $0<s\le1$. Let a sequence of pair laws of bounded planar parameters $(A,B)$ be dominated, with subpower loss at precision $\rho$, by the product of two populations having conditional child bounds of rate $s$. Assume that for each fixed $t>0$, both comparison marginals satisfy $$\label{eq:rate-no-strip}
 \sup_{\ell}\mu\{\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(\cdot,\ell)\le\rho^t\}
       \le\rho^{\kappa_t+o(1)}\quad\text{for some }\kappa_t>0,$$ and likewise for the other marginal. Suppose the direction-prefix probabilities conditional on either pin are homogenized on the same pair law. For each finite test, assume a common event of subpower pair mass on which the required original direction bins are uniform and the pair support lies over the simultaneous tested good-parent fibers for the child bounds. Then, on uniform trims, the direction of $B-A$ conditional on either pin has prefix ball dimension at least $s$. The assertion is hereditary under subpower restrictions which retain the stated child bounds and product comparisons.

Fix $0\le r<t$, a shape $0\le b\le a\le\min(L-t,u_t)$, and $0\le b_*\le b$. Choose $0<\eta<(u_r-b_*)/4$, and make the decomposition and cap-class construction of Section <a href="#sec:packet-structure" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:packet-structure">9</a>, with the mass floor <a href="#eq:composition-mass-floor" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:composition-mass-floor">[eq:composition-mass-floor]</a>. Within the localizer $J$, including its auxiliary tags, let $N_{\rm all}(J)$ be the size of the whole cap class, $N(J)>0$ its retained size, $m(J)$ its coefficient mass bin, and $g(J)$ its cap maximum bin. Set $$\begin{aligned}
 A_J&=\log_{1/\epsilon}\!\left(N(J)\right)+\tfrac32\log_{1/\epsilon}\!\left(m(J)\right)+\tfrac12g(J),
       \label{eq:composition-A}\\
 K_r^*&=\mathsf H(J)+b_*+
       \mathbb E\left[\log_{1/\epsilon}\!\left(N_{\rm all}(J)\right)
                    +\tfrac32\log_{1/\epsilon}\!\left(m(J)\right)+\tfrac12g(J)\right].
       \label{eq:composition-Kstar}
\end{aligned}$$ Let $K_J$ denote the canonical local root score, including its norm option, for the retained local superposition. Let $P_J$ be its score at duration $t-r$, with the conditional output law and the winning superposition masses. All expectations here use the current marginal of $J$ after the indicated preliminary split.*




*With $\mathcal R$ as in <a href="#eq:composition-error-budget" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:composition-error-budget">[eq:composition-error-budget]</a>, enlarged by a fixed constant if necessary, the following four quantities are nonnegative: $$\begin{aligned}
 d_0&=K_0+\beta_{\mathrm q}r-K_r^*+2\mathcal R,\nonumber\\
 d_1(J)&=\log_{1/\epsilon}\!\left(N_{\rm all}(J)/N(J)\right),\nonumber\\
 d_2(J)&=A_J-K_J+\mathcal R,\nonumber\\
 d_3(J)&=K_J+\beta_{\mathrm q}(t-r)-P_J+\mathcal R.
       \label{eq:four-packet-deficits}
\end{aligned}$$ Up to the displayed error allowances, these are the deficits in growth before the cut, class retention, the local root comparison, and growth after the cut. The next inequality shows that saturation at the endpoint forces these four comparisons to be nearly sharp on average; the subsequent selection estimate specifies the branches on which this remains true. Writing $\Delta_t=K_0+\beta_{\mathrm q}t-P_t$, one has the finite bound $$\label{eq:finite-packet-deficits}
 d_0+\mathbb E(d_1+d_2+d_3)\le\Delta_t+5\mathcal R.$$ There is also an earlier test on the *retained uniform classes*, with that same current marginal of $J$ and the assigned cap labels, whose score satisfies $$\label{eq:retained-back-score}
 P_r^{\rm ret}\ge K_r^*
              -\tfrac32\mathbb Ed_1-\mathcal R.$$ At $r=0$ this is an actual test for the original canonical $K_0$; the norm option is not used in this lower bound.*


*More quantitatively, put $R_t=\Delta_t+5\mathcal R$. For an event $B$ measurable in $J$ with probability $p>0$ and any $\theta>0$, $$\label{eq:packet-typical-selection}
 \mathbb P\bigl(B\cap\{d_1+d_2+d_3>\theta\}\bigr)
       \le R_t/\theta.$$ In particular, taking $\theta=2R_t/p$ leaves at least $p/2$ of the current probability in $B$, and bounds each of the three local deficits by $2R_t/p$. If $R_t=0$, all three vanish almost surely.

Assume Theorem <a href="#thm:classical-growth" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:classical-growth">15</a> and $\beta_{\mathrm q}>\gamma/2$. A saturating packet law at any fixed positive duration has $$\label{eq:active-packet-boundary}
 a=u_t+o(1)$$ on its typical fixed-shape branches. More precisely, if $r=t-2h>0$ and $a+2h\le u_r-2\eta$, the construction above with $b_*=b$ gives $$\label{eq:off-boundary-finite-growth}
 P_t\le K_0+\beta_{\mathrm q}r+\gamma h
              +C\mathcal R+\tfrac12\omega^{\rm cl}_{\mathcal B}(\epsilon).$$ Here $\omega^{\rm cl}_{\mathcal B}\to0$ is the fixed-class error in the classical growth theorem, and the constant is fixed for this cut.

Fix bounded input and output regions $K_v,K_x\subset\mathbb R^2$. For the input patch $K_v$, fix a class of smooth real phases satisfying the phase assumptions of Section <a href="#sec:packet-structure" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:packet-structure">9</a>, with common domain $[0,1]\times\mathbb R^2\times U_v$, where $U_v$ is an open neighborhood of $K_v$. All enlarged patches required by the decomposition, expressed in the original $v$ coordinates, are contained in $U_v$ and obey common bounds. The translated systems are considered on the corresponding bounded rescaled patches described in that section, with the resulting uniform bounds. Throughout the phase domain, $$\partial_v\Phi(c,x,v)=\zeta(v,x-cv).$$ For each phase and each required original input patch $V$, the matrix $\partial_p\zeta$ is symmetric and satisfies either $$a_0I\le\partial_p\zeta(v,p)\le a_1I
 \qquad(v\in V,\ p\in\mathbb R^2),$$ or the same inequality with $-\partial_p\zeta$ in place of $\partial_p\zeta$, with common constants $a_0>0$ and $a_1$ for the class. On these original patches, every mixed $v,p$ derivative of total order $j$ of this matrix is bounded by $A^{j+1}(j!)^3$ for a common $A\ge1$. Fix these constants and all the seminorm bounds of a class of smooth amplitudes $B$ supported in $[0,1]\times K_x\times K_v$. For every $\nu>0$ there is a constant $C_\nu$, depending only on these fixed data and $\nu$, such that, for every $\lambda\ge2$, every phase and amplitude in the specified classes, and every $f\in L^3(\mathbb R^2)$ supported in $K_v$, $$\label{eq:oscillatory-estimate}
 \left\|E_\lambda f\right\|_{L^3([0,1]\times K_x)}
 \le C_\nu\lambda^{-1+\nu}\|f\|_3,
 \qquad
 E_\lambda f(c,x)=\int e^{i\lambda\Phi(c,x,v)}B(c,x,v)f(v)\,dv.$$

The kernel is bounded on $\mathbb R^3$. For $R\ge1$ it has an expansion $$\label{eq:br-kernel-symbol}
 K_\delta(Re_3)=e^{2\pi iR}b_+(R)+e^{-2\pi iR}b_-(R),
 \qquad
 |b_\pm^{(j)}(R)|\le C_{\delta,j}R^{-2-\delta-j}
 \quad(j\ge0).$$ In particular $K_\delta\in L^1(\mathbb R^3)$ whenever $\delta>1$.

For $\Re z>0$ and $R>0$, let $T_{z,R}$ have multiplier $(1-|\xi|^2/R^2)_+^z$, and write $T_z=T_{z,1}$. Suppose $r>0$ and $T_r$ is bounded on $L^q$ for $1\le q<\infty$. If $a>r$ and $N$ is an integer with $N>r+1$, then $$\label{eq:br-complex-bound}
 \|T_{a+i\tau}\|_{q\to q}
 \le \|T_r\|_{q\to q}
 \prod_{k=0}^{N-1}\left(1+\frac{\tau^2}{(a-r+k)^2}\right)^{1/2}.$$ The same conclusion holds for $q=\infty$ if $K_r\in L^1$.
